          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11. Two friends are standing along a steel fence at a distance of 340 m from each other (Fig. 10.23). Gunjan places her ear over the fence and her friend knocks the fence with a metal object. Using the values of the speed of sound in steel and air given in Table 10.1, calculate the time difference between the sound that reached Gunjan through the air"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.109,
            0.857,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.548,
                0.109,
                0.857,
                0.199
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.561,
                0.204,
                0.839,
                0.234
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 10.23: Time difference between the sound reaching through air and steel"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.243,
            0.862,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and the steel. Would it have been possible for her to distinguish between the two sounds? (The time interval between two sounds must be at least 0.1 s to be heard separately.)"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.331,
            0.609,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10.6.4 Human perception of sound"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.353,
            0.89,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The physical properties of sound, such as time period, wavelength, frequency, amplitude and speed are well-defined and can be measured. However, how we experience sound is subjective. Human perception of sound is described by terms, such as loudness and pitch which do not have simple relations with the physical properties."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.447,
            0.285,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pitch"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.468,
            0.89,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How frequency is perceived by humans is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pitch",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Sounds perceived to be shrill, such as a whistle or a siren are said to have high pitch, while deep sounds like thunder or an aircraft rumble have low pitch. In general, high pitch sounds have higher frequency and low pitch sounds have lower frequency, although the exact mathematical relation is complicated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.561,
            0.236,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.166,
                0.561,
                0.236,
                0.599
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.245,
            0.573,
            0.462,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.6,
            0.863,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Everyone’s voice sounds unique and you can often instantly recognise your teacher or friends calling out your name. Why do our voices sound different? Male, female, and children’s voices differ not just in their frequency but also on how the sound is shaped by the throat, mouth, and nasal cavities. During adolescence, boys’ vocal cords of boys lengthen and thicken, vibrating less frequently thus, ‘deepening’ their voice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.706,
            0.888,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us carry out an activity to listen and appreciate sounds at different frequencies."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.749,
            0.81,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 10.8: Let us experiment (demonstration activity)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.771,
            0.887,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This activity is recommended to be performed as a classroom group activity facilitated by teacher."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.808,
            0.668,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Open a mobile app that can generate sounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.828,
            0.785,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Set the frequency to 100 Hz, tap ‘play’, and listen carefully."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.849,
            0.888,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Increase the frequency in steps of 100 Hz up to 1000 Hz and describe how the sound changes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.886,
            0.888,
            0.922
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Next, set the frequency to 50 Hz. Reduce the frequency till about 20 Hz or the point where you cannot hear the sound anymore."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
